  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nRest of Today - Monday: Dry and Seasonable\n\nWe find ourselves solidly embedded in northwesterly flow this \nafternoon as an upper tropospheric trough digs over the Great \nLakes/Ohio River valley and a paired meridional ridge builds \nalong the Front Range. This upstream ridge slowly migrates \neastward over the next 24-48 hours, with the ridge axis over the\nHigh Plains on Sunday and central Missouri River Valley by \nmidday Monday. The net result for the region will be continued \ndry and pleasant conditions through Monday. Highs should top out\nin the upper 70s to low 80s with a Canadian fetch dropping \ndewpoints into the 40s and 50s. Not bad for late August!\n\nThe only forecast concerns for this timeframe will revolve \naround any overnight/early morning valley fog, which looks to be\nconfined to the Mississippi River tributaries tonight but could\nbecome more widespread Sunday night depending on how the upper \nlevel clouds pan out.\n\nTuesday - Wednesday: Storm Potential Returns\n\nMedium range guidance continues to be in good agreement that \nour main window for precipitation in the seven day forecast will\ncome between Tuesday and Wednesday mornings as a compact vort \nlobe crests the aforementioned ridge and slides across Minnesota\nand Wisconsin. There may be stronger mid to upper level \nunidirectional flow with this complex that could result in \norganized cell structures should the lower level thermodynamic \nfields line up. Early looks at some of the deterministic GFS \nforecast soundings reflect this notion with elongated NW to SE \nhodographs that would support all modes of severe convection. \nThe devil is in the details at this stage in the forecast and, \ngiven the wide range of plausible outcomes, plenty can change \nover the next 2-3 days.\n\nLatter Half of the Week: Dry Start, More Active for the Weekend\n\nWe return to northwesterly flow and dry conditions for the \nlatter half of the week with no real signal for additional \nrainfall until next weekend as the ridge flattens and several \nwaves push through. There is some signal for a more organized \nprecipitation event in some of the longer range deterministic \nand ensemble solutions with some outliers depicting swaths of \n1-2+ inches of rain for later in the weekend. That being said, \nthese are the outlier (<10%) solutions and there is a wide \nspread in the track of this system at this range.",
